From c4aa6182c560ce9e324ebfb84b3f8f44ad8f80ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 22:24:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Engine: "i in array" was a C++ call, once per element

Every array method that takes a callback runs it. ArrayPrototype.js has
`if (!(i in array)) continue;` inside forEach, map, filter, every, some,
reduce and find, and op_in was a DEFINE_SLOW_OP - no fast path on any
architecture, a full call through slow_path_in with a throw scope, per
element, to answer what is usually a bounds check and a tag compare.

Worth 27% on a kernel of map and filter over 4,000 elements: 821ms to
599ms on the PowerBook G4, and 9.2% off the whole kernel set.

The fast path is narrower than emit_op_has_indexed_property, which it is
modelled on. That opcode is emitted by for-in and may assume an integer
index; "foo" in obj is legal, so a property that is not an int32 goes
slow. It also does without the ByValInfo patching machinery, which
exists so a get_by_val site can be repatched for a different array
shape - here the shapes that matter are checked inline.

Only a hit is answered inline. Out of bounds, or a hole, falls to the
slow path: "absent from this object" is not the same question as
"absent", and the prototype chain may still have it.

**The double case is the one that pays, and the first version did not
have it.** A first cut handled Int32Shape and ContiguousShape only, and
measured 1.3% *slower* than no fast path at all - an array of
measurements is double-shaped, so every `in` still went slow, now behind
ten instructions of shape checks that could not match. That is also the
shape a charting library plots, so the version that looked finished
would have done nothing for the one Speedometer suite this was aimed at.
DoubleShape marks a hole with NaN rather than an empty tag and needs its
own bounds and hole check, which is the difference between -1.3% and
-27%.

Gated to JSVALUE32_64. Other value layouts keep the slow op.

Correctness: 25 cases covering dense hits, out of bounds, negative and
fractional indices, holes, string keys, prototype chain lookups,
"length", an index spelled as a string, and double and typed arrays that
must take the slow path and still answer correctly - each run 20,000
times to force tier-up, since the fast path only exists in compiled
code. The 78-check probe is unchanged at 72 pass, 6 fail, the same six
BigInt features 604 does not have.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Source/JavaScriptCore/jit/JIT.cpp             |  7 ++
 Source/JavaScriptCore/jit/JIT.h               |  6 ++
 Source/JavaScriptCore/jit/JITOpcodes32_64.cpp | 86 +++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 99 insertions(+)

